  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/17e6320115f2cf8b5ffad6abc099169774536af20d2559b453aaa97c1286d96d.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{24}}{7} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos\u2061\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{6}}=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}.\n$$",
